  "theorem_name": "RelativeChenDefectQuantitativeTwinStrengthCalibration",
  "declared_proposition": "For every dyadic block, delta_X=1-L2(X)/C2(X)=2T(X)/C2(X) exactly. Therefore, if C2(X)>=a X/(log_2 X)^2 and delta_X>=d>0 on infinitely many blocks, then T(X)>=ad X/(2(log_2 X)^2) on those blocks. A fixed relative defect plus Chen-order mass is a Hardy-Littlewood-order quantitative Twin lower bound, strictly stronger than mere Twin infinitude. Channel algebra alone cannot derive it: the exact abstract model T=1, C2=floor(X/log_2^2 X), L2=C2-2 is twin-positive on every block while delta_X tends to zero.",
  "mathematical_argument": "The projector identity gives C2-L2=2T, so division by C2 proves delta_X=2T/C2. Multiplying a Chen-mass lower bound by d/2 gives the stated twin lower bound. In the abstract model, C2-L2=2 and T=1 exactly on every block, but delta_X=2/C2 tends to zero as C2 grows. Thus infinitude and a fixed relative defect are not equivalent consequences of the channel identities.",
